<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Tool Bridging: Relation to Prior Work on Specialized Tools</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary contribution is the explicit validation of two principles for agent design. First, replacing a general-purpose <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shell</span> with a curated set of domain-aware tools improves performance. Second, this performance gain increases as the specificity of the tools increases (Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.1 Impact of domain-specific tools ‣ 4 Results and Discussions ‣ Automating Android Build Repair: Bridging the Reasoning-Execution Gap in LLM Agents with Domain-Specific Tools" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). We term this strategy <span id="A1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tool Bridging</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While we are inspired by prior work that successfully employs specialized tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, their focus differs from ours. In prior work, such tools are a foundational component for achieving a broader goal, such as lifelong learning or deep code analysis. In contrast, our work isolates the act of tool replacement itself as the central research question, empirically demonstrating its efficacy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distinction from Voyager <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</span>
The Voyager agent is an influential paper for lifelong learning within an embodied environment (Minecraft). It operates by building a growing skill library from executable JavaScript functions which call predefined, domain-specific APIs like <span id="A1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mineBlock(bot, name)</span>. These APIs form the agent’s fundamental action space.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The key distinction is that Voyager’s contribution is not a study of these tools against a general alternative. The paper’s novelties are the learned skill library and iterative prompting. The specialized APIs are a prerequisite for the agent to function. However, the paper does not present an ablation where the agent uses a general Minecraft command-line shell, nor does it test if more specific tools would further improve performance. In contrast, our work directly compares an agent with a general <span id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shell</span> against one with domain-specific tools (GradleFixer), attributing the performance gain to the <span id="A1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tool Bridging</span> strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distinction from Code Researcher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</span>
Code Researcher is a powerful agent designed to patch crashes in complex systems code. It uses specialized tools for code analysis, such as <span id="A1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search_code(regex)</span> and <span id="A1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search_commits(regex)</span>, which are domain-aware for code and history investigation.
Similar to Voyager, the paper’s contribution is its deep research methodology, not a comparative analysis of its tools. The research question is not "Is search_commits better than git log in a shell?" but rather "How can an agent use a specialized tool to leverage codebase history to solve complex bugs?". Conversely, our work focuses directly on this comparison. We provide evidence that abstracting complex commands into a constrained, API-like action space is a key strategy for improving agent performance in the Android build domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This distinction also explains a key aspect of our experimental design. The goal of our agent is to repair a build error, not simply to identify the commit that introduced it. Because our dataset consists of build-breaking commits, a history-aware tool would allow the agent to solve the problem by reverting the change. We excluded this capability to keep the task focused on code repair.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Scope and Limitations of Our Contribution</h3>

<div id="A1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work is a careful study that specialized tools improve agent performance, with empirical validation in the Android build domain. Demonstrating generalizability to other domains is important future work. Furthermore, our results provide strong empirical evidence that API-like formatting and action space constraint contribute to Tool Bridging’s effectiveness. However, disentangling the precise cognitive mechanisms of the LLM remains a challenge, and our claims are based on inferences from agent performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Comparison of the official Gemini-CLI agent and our Python replica. Both agents were configured with only the <span id="A1.T9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shell</span> tool. The similar pass@1 rates validate the accuracy of our replica.</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T9.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T9.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T9.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T9.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation</span></td>
<td id="A1.T9.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T9.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pass@1 (%)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T9.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T9.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Original Gemini-CLI</td>
<td id="A1.T9.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">65.1</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T9.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T9.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Our Python Replica</td>
<td id="A1.T9.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">65.7</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Validation of the Gemini-CLI Replica</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we required the ability to systematically modify the toolset available to the agent to isolate the impact of domain-specific tools. To facilitate these controlled experiments, we developed a Python replica of the open-source Gemini-CLI agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google Cloud, </a>)</cite>. Our replica preserves the core agentic loop and prompting structure of the original implementation while allowing for straightforward customization of the available tools.
To validate the fidelity of our replica, we conducted an experiment to compare its performance against the official, unmodified Gemini-CLI agent. For a fair comparison, both agents were configured to use only the general-purpose <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shell</span> tool, in addition to standard file system tools. This configuration tests whether our replica preserves the capabilities of the original agent framework.
The results of this validation are shown in Table <a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Scope and Limitations of Our Contribution ‣ Appendix A Tool Bridging: Relation to Prior Work on Specialized Tools ‣ Automating Android Build Repair: Bridging the Reasoning-Execution Gap in LLM Agents with Domain-Specific Tools" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. We find that the resolve rates are nearly identical. This small difference is well within the expected variance due to model stochasticity. Temperature is kept at the default of 1. The close alignment in performance confirms that our replica is an accurate and reliable reproduction of the original agent, validating its use for the <span id="A2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini-CLI (Read/Write Only)</span> and <span id="A2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GradleFixer</span> configurations in our experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Prompts</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>System prompt</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the long length of the system prompt, we point readers to the open-source Gemini-CLI repository on GitHub (<span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">packages/core/src/core/prompts.ts</span>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Initial prompt</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following is the initial prompt that is given to the LLM agents.</p>
</div>
